<h2>2. What data we hold</h2>
<p>The list below is not a summary written from memory: it mirrors the technical inventory the platform uses to build your data export — every database table that can concern you, and the file of the archive it goes into. Anything we hold about you appears in one of these categories.</p>

<h3>2.1 Identity and profile</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>First name, last name, display name, alias</td><td>Required to appear on a roster and on results</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Email address</td><td>Required on an event roster; used for login and notifications. <strong>Never published.</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Password</td><td>Never stored in clear text — only a cryptographic hash held by our authentication service</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Date of birth, gender category</td><td>Optional; used for age and category eligibility</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Club, country</td><td>Displayed alongside results, as at the venue</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Profile photo</td><td>Optional, uploaded by you or by the organiser</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HEMA Ratings identifier</td><td>Optional; links your MyClash profile to your public HEMA Ratings profile</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Social and personal links, weapon disciplines, honours</td><td>Optional, entered by you</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Notes taken by the organiser</td><td>Free text an organiser may attach to their own roster row</td></tr>
    <tr><td>About you as a workshop's instructor</td><td>A bio, a club line and a photo that the organiser, or you, may write for one workshop. Shown on that workshop's public page in place of your profile's; deleted when you are taken off the workshop, and when your account is erased</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>2.2 Competition record</h3>
<p>Registrations, pools, brackets, Swiss rounds, seeding, bouts fought, exchange-by-exchange scoring, penalties and cards received, forfeits, rankings, league standings and placements.</p>

<h3>2.3 Roles at an event</h3>
<p>Referee qualifications and per-role ratings, availability, assignments to pistes and bouts, referee compensation and payment records, workshop instruction and enrolment, feedback given on workshops.</p>

<h3>2.4 Account activity</h3>
<p>Organisation memberships and roles, the people and organisers you follow, your directory groups, notification preferences, browser push subscriptions, requests you make (profile claim, email change, correction of an exchange, league membership, club review), and your privacy settings, and the photos you sent from your personal space (see §3bis).</p>

<h3>2.5 Record of your agreement to our documents</h3>
<p>When you create an account, or continue as a guest, we record <strong>which version</strong> of the Terms of Service and of this Privacy Policy was displayed to you, <strong>when</strong> you accepted it, and the <strong>IP address and browser</strong> the acceptance came from. We are required to be able to demonstrate consent, and a timestamp alone would not do that. These records are never modified — accepting a revised version adds a new record beside the old one.</p>

<h3>2.6 Assistance features (AI)</h3>
<p>If an organiser has enabled the AI features (see §7), the questions asked and the content generated are recorded, together with the volume consumed. See §8 for what leaves our infrastructure.</p>

<h3>2.7 Technical data</h3>
<p>The IP address and browser of a guest session and of an acceptance record, server logs, and error reports. Server logs record each request: the address it came from, what was asked for, and when. Each service keeps them in a limited space where newer lines replace the oldest, so the busier the platform, the sooner a line goes: at an event, usually within a few days. They are not in our backups, and we use them only to keep the platform working and secure. <strong>We do not perform audience measurement</strong>: MyClash contains no analytics tool, no advertising tag, and no third-party tracker. Apart from these logs and the error reports of §7.1, we keep no record of the pages you visit, and nothing measures how long you stay on them.</p>

<h3>2.8 What we never collect</h3>
<p>No special-category data within the meaning of Article 9 GDPR (health, ethnic origin, political or religious opinions, biometric data), no bank or payment details, and no precise geolocation.</p>

<!-- 3 -->
<h2>3. Being on MyClash without ever having signed up</h2>
<p>This is the most distinctive part of the platform, and it deserves a section of its own.</p>
<p>To run a competition, an organiser enters their roster: everyone taking part, with the details listed in §2.1. <strong>You can therefore exist on MyClash without ever having created an account</strong>, because the organiser needed to register you in order to run their event.</p>
<p>In that situation:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You may claim your profile.</strong> A link sent to the email address the organiser registered proves it is you, and the profile becomes yours.</li>
  <li><strong>You may use a guest session.</strong> Finding your own name on an event's roster opens a limited session — your schedule, your bouts, your workshops — with no account and no password. It is a cookie tied to that event which expires seven days after the event ends.</li>
  <li><strong>You may object.</strong> Write to the organiser, or to us; see §11.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>You may also put yourself on an event's list.</strong> When an organiser allows it for an event, someone with a MyClash account who is not on its list can add themselves from one of its workshop pages. The entry carries the name on your profile (or the one you type, if your profile has none) and your account's email address. It shows in the event's participants list like any other entry, and the organiser sees it and can remove it. Nothing else is collected.</p>

<!-- 3bis -->
<h2 id="photos">3bis. An event's photos</h2>
<p>An organiser may open their event to photos: people at the venue send them from their phone, and the event's public page shows them. The feature is off by default; it exists on an event only if its organiser turned it on.</p>
<p>The organiser and LYON AMHE are jointly responsible for these photos; §1.1 says who does what.</p>

<h3>What we record</h3>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Photo sent…</th><th>What we record</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>from the QR code</strong>, with no account</td><td>The photo; a name and a caption, if you type them (optional); the tournament or workshop you chose; the time it was sent; a random number for your phone, stored with the photo and kept in the <code>mc_photo_device</code> cookie (see §9). The photo is not stored with your account, even if you are signed in.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>from your personal space</strong>, signed in</td><td>The photo, your caption, the tournament or workshop you chose, the time, and <strong>your name</strong> — your profile's, or else the one you typed when you signed up —, shown under the photo. The personal space has no anonymous sending: use the QR code for that.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Before we store a photo, we remove everything the phone hides in it: <strong>the GPS position</strong>, the device model, the date it was taken, thumbnails. Only the orientation is kept, so the photo stays upright. The picture itself is not retouched; we also keep a smaller copy for display. No face recognition is applied. The photo does not carry your IP address; like every visit, sending it leaves a line in our server logs (see §2.7).</p>

<h3>Who sees them</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Depending on the organiser's choice, a photo is <strong>public</strong> on the event's page as soon as it is sent, or only <strong>once it is approved</strong>. A photo waiting for approval is visible to the moderators only, and to its sender in their personal space if they sent it from there.</li>
  <li>The <strong>moderators</strong> are the organising club's administrators, the volunteers it gives the photos to, and MyClash's platform administrators, who step in to answer requests and stop abuse. They see every photo of the event and its reports, and may delete any of them.</li>
  <li>The organiser may <strong>download the event's photos</strong> as one zip archive — published ones and those still waiting —, with the list of names, captions, tournaments or workshops, times and statuses, until they are deleted. <strong>The downloaded copies are then the organiser's sole responsibility</strong>: the automatic deletion below does not reach them.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Reporting a photo</h3>
<p>Under every public photo, "Report" puts it in the list of reported photos the moderators see. We record the comment you add (optional, at most 200 characters) and your phone's random number (the same cookie). The photo stays visible until a moderator deletes it or decides to keep it; if they keep it, the reports they read are erased.</p>

<h3>How long</h3>
<p><strong>Every photo of an event is deleted 30 days after the event's last day</strong>, at midnight, event time, with its name, caption and reports. A photo goes earlier if a moderator deletes it or the event is deleted; and, for a photo sent from your personal space, if you delete it, delete your account, or ask us to anonymise your name. The images are never in our backups; the names, captions and reports stay in our encrypted database backups for about 60 more days (see §6.1).</p>

<h3>What you can do</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You sent a photo from your personal space</strong>: the event's Photos tab, in your personal space, lists it, and you can delete it at any time. If the event no longer shows in your personal space, write to us.</li>
  <li><strong>You sent a photo from the QR code</strong>: it is not stored with your account, so you cannot delete it yourself. Ask the organiser, or write to us.</li>
  <li><strong>You appear in a photo</strong> you do not want shown: press "Report" and say so, or write to the organiser. If you cannot reach them, write to us (see §11): we will help.</li>
  <li><strong>A photo shows your child</strong>: as their parent or guardian, ask for it to be removed in the same way (Report, the organiser, or us). We remove it without asking you for a reason.</li>
</ul>

