<h2>8. Transfers outside the European Union</h2>
<p>Our infrastructure — application, database, files, backups — is hosted <strong>entirely in France</strong>. Two flows nonetheless leave the European Union, and we state them plainly rather than claiming otherwise:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Flow</th><th>Destination</th><th>Safeguard</th><th>Can you avoid it?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Push notifications</td><td>Your browser's push service</td><td>The provider's own framework</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — do not enable notifications, or revoke them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI requests</td><td>The provider chosen by the organiser</td><td>Standard Contractual Clauses. Mistral AI is French, so that option stays within the EU</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — the feature is off unless an organiser enables it</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>A note on Sentry</strong>, which appeared in this table until 11 August 2026: our error logs are sent to Sentry's <strong>European region</strong> and stored in Germany, not in the United States. They are therefore not a transfer outside the European Union. Sentry is still published by an American company, Functional Software, Inc.: access from the United States by its support staff remains possible, governed by the Standard Contractual Clauses in its data processing agreement. Their only personal data is the IP address of the browser that sent a report or a trace — see §7.1.</p>

